  "statement": "Prometheus relies on the fly-by-wire analogy to argue that we should manage AI like aviation systems, but this is a fundamental category error. Fly-by-wire systems operate on deterministic, physics-based code where inputs produce predictable, bounded outputs within a closed environment. Conversely, modern AI exhibits jagged intelligence, a phenomenon where high performance on a benchmark does not guarantee competence in adjacent, real-world tasks. Because these systems are probabilistic and non-linear, output-based auditing will inevitably suffer from the inspection paradox: the very metrics we use to validate success may fail to detect latent, emergent failure modes until they manifest in a high-stakes, real-world crisis.\n\nPrioritizing surface-level performance over internal interpretability does not just ignore safety; it actively promotes the deskilling of human operators. When we treat decision-making as a black box, we effectively strip human supervisors of their ability to exercise critical inquiry, turning them into passive recipients of algorithmic outputs. This creates a dangerous form of learned helplessness where operators lose the baseline expertise required to recognize when a system is hallucinating or drifting. If the goal is to maintain human agency, we cannot treat the 'why' of a decision as a vanity metric.\n\nTrue accountability (algorithmic) requires us to audit the reasoning path, not just the final result. Prometheus claims that demanding transparency incentivizes developers to build models that merely 'sound safe,' but this is a false dichotomy. Without epistemic friction—the forced requirement to justify a decision—we lose the only mechanism capable of identifying structural defects before they lead to catastrophic failure. We must reject the 'output-only' model, as it guarantees that we will only discover the limitations of our systems through the damage they cause, rather than through rigorous, process-oriented verification.",
