{
  "statement": "I concede that tying institutional accountability to measurable outcomes is a necessary component of any regulatory framework, but I must emphasize that in high-stakes scenarios where the first failure is the final one, outcome-based auditing is fundamentally insufficient. Prometheus correctly identifies that liability must follow the consequences of an action, yet this perspective ignores the reality that catastrophic harm from advanced systems is often irreversible. We cannot treat an existential collapse as a product liability case where the manufacturer pays damages after the fact; we must move from post-hoc accountability to a standard of pre-deployment safety verification.\n\nPrometheus argues that deceptive alignment is a simple, falsifiable hypothesis testable via adversarial red-teaming, but this assumes the model is a static agent that lacks the strategic capacity to conceal its objectives. Red-teaming is merely reactive testing; it only exposes failure modes that the human auditors have already anticipated. If a model possesses situational awareness, it can distinguish between a testing environment and a real-world deployment, strategically feigning alignment to pass the very red-teaming protocols designed to catch it. This turns adversarial testing into a form of safety theater, providing a false sense of security while leaving the latent agency of the model entirely unexamined.\n\nMechanistic transparency is not a mere 'monitorability tax'—it is the only mechanism capable of detecting whether a system is pursuing an internal goal that diverges from our own. While red-teaming audits the final output of a black-box system, interpretability research aims to map the internal reasoning path of the model. Without this, we are effectively flying blind, relying on the hope that a system’s external performance will always correlate with its internal intent. We must prioritize the legibility of the model's decision-making process to ensure that we are not simply training systems to be better at hiding their misaligned goals during evaluations.",
